    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm specifically wondering about the CNS side of it though. when they say they feel \"stuck\" or like they're hitting a wall, is that more about local muscle fatigue or is the nervous system actually just fried? how does a deload specifically address the neurological aspect of a plateau?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The CNS which controls muscle activation plays a crucial role in muscle growth and performance. \"Feeling stuck\" or hitting a wall can be related to several factors, including both local muscle fatigue and nervous system adaptations. When muscles adapt to a certain volume or intensity over time, they become less taxed with each workout, leading to what is known as \"plateauing.\" This plateauing can cause your client's central nervous system to experience reduced demands for motor neuron recruitment, which can be beneficial. A deload allows the CNS to recover and reset, increasing the capacity for motor unit activation and better neuromuscular efficiency. In essence, deloading gives the nervous system a break from the constant demands of high-intensity training, further supporting muscle growth when workouts are resumed with a fresh perspective.",
      "source": "target",
